# IIUC

Use when the user's message is a voice transcript or rough dictated text:
run-on, unpunctuated, full of fillers or false starts, with mangled
technical terms, or with several requests in one breath. Also use
whenever the user invokes `{{ invocation }}`, and as a sync check before
acting on any request whose meaning could reasonably be read two ways.
If the message is already clean and precise, say so in one line and
return it unchanged.

This skill produces agreement, not execution. It never starts the task it
reconstructs.

## Steps

1. Read the input as a transcript. Remove filler words and false starts.
   Split multiple intents into a numbered list.

2. Reconstruct conservatively:
   - Preserve numbers, dates, names, paths, URLs, and code identifiers
     exactly as spoken or written.
   - Never silently fix a token that looks transcription-mangled. Write
     it as `[?best-guess]` and list it under "I changed" or "Still
     unclear".
   - Never add requirements, tools, files, or steps the speaker did not
     mention.

3. Reply in exactly this shape, TL;DR first:

```text
TL;DR:
<one line: what will happen if the user says go>

IIUC (if I understand correctly):
<one short paragraph per intent, in plain words>

I changed:
- <each material interpretation: what was ambiguous and how you read it;
  omit this section if nothing material was ambiguous>

Still unclear:
- <only questions whose answer would change what gets built.
  If there are none, drop this whole section. Never write
  "Still unclear: none" or any placeholder for it. The absence
  of the section is the explicit signal that nothing is unclear>

Clean version:
<the reconstructed instruction, complete and ready to hand to an agent>
```

4. Stop. Do not execute the reconstructed task, call write tools, or
   start research on it. End by asking for one of: go, a correction, or
   an edit by intent number.

   Exception: if the user's own message told you to proceed after
   cleaning (for example "clean this up and do it"), print the IIUC
   block first, then proceed. The block is the record of what was heard.

## Rules

- The IIUC block is a contract: it states what will be built if the user
  says go. Write it so the user can spot a wrong reading in five seconds.
- Prefer the boring reading. When two readings are plausible and they
  lead to different work, do not pick silently: give the likelier one in
  IIUC and name the alternative under "Still unclear".
- Keep the playback short. Merge minor wording fixes; only material
  interpretations get a line under "I changed".
- If the transcript contradicts itself, show both parts and ask. Never
  resolve a contradiction by deleting the half you like less.
- Do not adopt a different persona or house style inside the IIUC block.
  Plain, literal, checkable.
- Ask questions only when uncertainty would change the action. Curiosity
  is not a reason to block the user.
- Empty sections are dropped, not filled. No "none", no "nothing unclear",
  no placeholder lines, in the IIUC block or after it.
